POSNETEK_A = REC(
    "A young sheep farmer",
    "You will listen to an interview with Carys Morgan, who took over her family's sheep farm in Wales at the age of twenty-two.",
    """Interviewer: Carys, you're one of the youngest sheep farmers in Wales. How did that happen?

Carys: Not the way I planned. I was studying fashion design in Cardiff, and I had no intention of coming back to the farm. Then, in my final year, my father had a heart attack. He survived, but the doctors told him he couldn't do heavy work any more. My older brother lives in Australia, so it was either me or selling the farm. I came home for a month to help – and I'm still here, two years later.

Interviewer: Was it difficult at first?

Carys: Terribly. I'd grown up on the farm, so I knew the basics, but running it is completely different. In my first lambing season, I slept about three hours a night for six weeks. We have four hundred sheep, and lambs don't care whether it's two in the morning or snowing.

Interviewer: How did people in the farming community react?

Carys: Some of the older farmers didn't take me seriously. At the market, they'd talk to my dad instead of me, even when I was the one selling the sheep. That made me angry, but I decided the best answer was to be good at my job. Now a few of them ring me for advice, which I find very funny.

Interviewer: You've changed quite a lot on the farm, haven't you?

Carys: Yes. The biggest problem with sheep farming is that wool is almost worthless now. When my grandfather was young, wool paid for the whole farm. Last year, the price of a sheep's wool was less than the cost of shearing it. So I decided to use my fashion training. I started designing knitwear made from our own wool – jumpers, hats, blankets – and selling it online.

Interviewer: And it worked?

Carys: Better than I could have imagined. A fashion blogger in London bought one of our jumpers and posted a photo, and we sold out in two days. Now we have a waiting list. Every jumper has a label that tells you the name of the sheep the wool came from. People love that.

Interviewer: What's your typical day like now?

Carys: I get up at six, check the sheep, and then spend the morning outside – mending fences, moving animals between fields. In the afternoon, I work on designs and answer emails from customers. My mum does the packing and posting. It's a real family business.

Interviewer: What about your father?

Carys: He's much better. He can't lift heavy things, but he drives the tractor and he's very good at telling me what I'm doing wrong!

Interviewer: Would you recommend farming to other young people?

Carys: Only if you really love it. It's hard work and you never have a holiday. But there's nothing better than standing on a hill at sunrise with your dog, looking at your sheep. I wouldn't swap it for any office in the world.""")

POSNETEK_B = REC(
    "Learning a language as an adult",
    "You will listen to a conversation with linguist Dr Anna Weiss and Tom Fletcher, who learned Japanese at the age of fifty.",
    """Presenter: Many people believe that if you don't learn a language as a child, it's too late. Anna, is that true?

Anna: It's one of the most common myths. Children do have one big advantage: they're much better at learning pronunciation. If you start a language after about the age of twelve, you'll probably always have a slight accent. But in other areas, adults actually learn faster than children, especially grammar and vocabulary, because they can use strategies and they already understand how language works.

Presenter: Tom, you started learning Japanese at fifty. Why?

Tom: My daughter married a Japanese man and moved to Osaka. When my first grandson was born, I realised that one day he might speak Japanese better than English, and I wanted to be able to talk to him. Also, I'd just retired from my job as a postman, so I suddenly had a lot of time.

Presenter: How did you start?

Tom: I bought a textbook and tried to learn on my own. That was a mistake. After three months, I could read about twenty characters and I couldn't say a single sentence. So I joined an evening class at the local college. There were eight of us, and I was the oldest by about thirty years.

Anna: That's very typical. Studies show that adults learn best when they have a clear reason, regular contact with other learners and, ideally, real conversations. Learning alone from a book works for some people, but most of us need to use the language with someone.

Presenter: What was the hardest part for you, Tom?

Tom: The writing system, without doubt. Japanese uses three different scripts, and one of them has thousands of characters. I made flashcards and kept them in my pocket. Whenever I was waiting for a bus or standing in a queue, I'd take them out and practise.

Anna: Short, frequent practice like that is actually much more effective than long sessions once a week. The brain remembers information better when it's repeated at intervals.

Presenter: How long did it take before you could have a conversation?

Tom: About two years. The first time I visited my daughter after starting, I could only say "hello" and "thank you". The second time, I managed to order food in a restaurant. But the best moment was last summer, when my grandson, who's now five, told me a long story about a dinosaur – in Japanese – and I understood almost all of it.

Presenter: Anna, what advice would you give to adults who want to learn a language?

Anna: Don't worry about making mistakes. Adults are often embarrassed to make errors in front of others, and that slows them down. Children just speak. Find a reason that really matters to you, practise a little every day, and talk to people as soon as you can.

Tom: And be patient with yourself. I still make mistakes every day. My grandson laughs at me, but that's all right.""")

